Goal: Use Ansible modules to check and manage your 2 EC2 servers.

You already have:

WSL
 |
 | Ansible
 |
 +---- EC2-1 (web1)
 |
 +---- EC2-2 (web2)

And your directory:

~/ansible-ec2-lab/
├── ansible.cfg
└── inventory

Step 1 — Check both servers

First:

cd ~/ansible-ec2-lab

Run:

web1 | SUCCESS => {
    "ping": "pong"
}

web2 | SUCCESS => {
    "ping": "pong"
}

Step 2 — Check disk usage

Run:

ansible all -m command -a "df -h /"

You'll get the root filesystem information from both servers.

For example:

Filesystem      Size  Used Avail Use%
/dev/root        8G   3G    5G   38%

Step 3 — Use the shell module

Now let's try another module.

ansible all -m shell -a "uptime && df -h /"

Here we're running two commands:

uptime

and:

df -h /

The shell module is useful when you need shell features or multiple commands.
ansible all -m ping

For basic single commands, command is generally preferred.

Step 7 — Create a file on both EC2 servers

Now let's actually change something.

Run:

ansible all -m file -a "path=/tmp/ansible-lab.txt state=touch"

This tells Ansible:

Create /tmp/ansible-lab.txt on every server.

Check it:

ansible all -m command -a "ls -l /tmp/ansible-lab.txt"

Step 8 — Put some text into the file

Use the copy module:

ansible all -m copy -a "content='Created by Ansible Lab 2\n' dest=/tmp/ansible-lab.txt"

Now check:

ansible all -m command -a "cat /tmp/ansible-lab.txt"

Expected:

Created by Ansible Lab 2

Step 9 — Install a package

This is a good real-world example.

Let's install tree:

ansible all -m apt -a "name=tree state=present" --become

=> What is --become?

It allows Ansible to execute the task with elevated privileges, similar to:

sudo

Because installing a package requires administrator/root privileges.

Then verify:

Then verify:

ansible all -m command -a "tree --version"

Step 10 — Check the package

You can also use:

ansible all -m command -a "which tree"

You should get:

/usr/bin/tree

Step 11 — Remove the test file

Clean up your lab:

ansible all -m file -a "path=/tmp/ansible-lab.txt state=absent"

Check:

ansible all -m command -a "ls -l /tmp/ansible-lab.txt"

It should say the file doesn't exist.


